# r_0(x) = x.
# r_{i}(x) = r_{i-1}(x) + 1 if L_i <= r_{i-1}(x) <= R_i, else r_{i-1}(x).
# If r_{i-1}(x+1) = r_{i-1}(x) + 1, then r_i(x+1) is either r_{i-1}(x+1) or r_{i-1}(x+1)+1.
# Since r_{i-1}(x+1) = r_{i-1}(x) + 1, r_i(x+1) is either r_{i-1}(x)+1 or r_{i-1}(x)+2.
# This logic is slightly flawed because r_{i-1}(x+1) could be equal to r_{i-1}(x).
# Let's re-evaluate: r_i(x) is a non-decreasing function of x.
# Let g_i(x) = r_i(x). g_0(x) = x.
# g_i(x) = g_{i-1}(x) + 1 if L_i <= g_{i-1}(x) <= R_i, else g_{i-1}(x).
# If g_{i-1}(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x), then g_i(x+1) = g_i(x).
# If g_{i-1}(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x) + 1, then:
#   Case 1: g_{i-1}(x) < L_i and g_{i-1}(x+1) < L_i => g_i(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x) + 1 = g_i(x) + 1.
#   Case 2: g_{i-1}(x) > R_i and g_{i-1}(x+1) > R_i => g_i(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x+1) = g_{i-1}(x) + 1 = g_i(x) + 1.
